From: Cubie A7S bringup <local>
Subject: [PATCH] sid: sunxi-sid: refuse the user-space eFuse write path (hardening)

An eFuse bit can only be programmed from 0 to 1 and can never be erased
(Allwinner 安全开发指南 2.2.4: "efuse 内部数据只能从 0 变成 1, 不能从 1 变成 0,
只能写入一次").  Writing the wrong secure-enable / ROTPK / SSK bit is therefore
irreversible and can permanently brick the SoC, so this path should not be
reachable from user space on a normal running system.

Vendor Android builds do enforce that: ueventd assigns explicit ownership to
the SID nodes (e.g. /dev/sunxi_soc_info 0640 media media) and SELinux denies
everything else -- /dev/sid_efuse is not even listed in file_contexts, so no
user-space domain can open it, and burning happens in recovery/factory mode
only.

On the Linux/Armbian side none of that exists: the runtime LSM list is just
"capability" (no SELinux/AppArmor; see
C-实测与调查/Armbian-SELinux-调查-20260918.md) and no udev rule touches these
nodes.  A udev rule was tried first and measured to be useless against the
actual threat:

    # /etc/udev/rules.d/99-sid-efuse.rules:  KERNEL=="sid_efuse", MODE="0000"
    $ sudo python3 -c "import os; os.close(os.open('/dev/sid_efuse', os.O_WRONLY))"  # succeeds
    $      python3 -c "same"                                                         # PermissionError

root's CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE defeats mode 0000, so the only effective place to close
this path is the kernel.

This patch refuses SUNXI_EFUSE_WRITE in the ioctl handler.  Only two places in
the whole BSP call arm_svc_efuse_write(): this ioctl branch and the internal
kernel API (sunxi-sid.c:949), and no other driver uses it -- so the user-space
write path is fully closed without affecting kernel functionality.  The read
path, ChipID and calibration reads are untouched.

The refusal uses `goto _out` on purpose: the function holds nfcr->mutex, and
_out is where the lock is released and the temporary buffers wiped -- a plain
`return -EPERM` would leak the lock.

The original write sequence is left in place (now unreachable) so that a plain
revert restores it verbatim.

Baseline: Allwinner A733 SDK vendor kernel, bsp/drivers/sid/sunxi-sid.c
  (2026-09-17 copy, md5 8f7a24a6e9959c429cf8679b9c8a4bfb).  Applies with
  `patch -p1` to the kernel tree used by the sun60iw2 vendor family.

Post-build check:
  dmesg | grep -i "efuse write is disabled"        # appears after a write attempt
  python3 -c "import os; os.close(os.open('/dev/sid_efuse', os.O_WRONLY))"
      # open() still succeeds (device exists), but the write ioctl now returns -EPERM
  # values read back via SUNXI_EFUSE_READ / ChipID / calibration are unchanged

---
diff --git a/bsp/drivers/sid/sunxi-sid.c b/bsp/drivers/sid/sunxi-sid.c
--- a/bsp/drivers/sid/sunxi-sid.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/sid/sunxi-sid.c
@@ -867,6 +867,24 @@
 		}
 		break;
 	case SUNXI_EFUSE_WRITE:
+		/*
+		 * Hardening (Cubie A7S / Armbian, see 0014): an eFuse bit can only
+		 * be programmed from 0 to 1 and can never be erased, so writing the
+		 * wrong secure-enable / ROTPK / SSK bit is irreversible and can
+		 * permanently brick the SoC.  DAC permissions cannot stop root
+		 * (CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE defeats mode 0000 -- verified on this board),
+		 * so the user-space write path is refused here, in the kernel.
+		 *
+		 * The read path, ChipID and calibration data are unaffected.
+		 * Factory programming needs a kernel without this change (or the
+		 * vendor's own burning tool under a permissive build).
+		 *
+		 * The original write sequence is kept below (now unreachable) so
+		 * that a plain revert restores it verbatim.
+		 */
+		pr_err("sunxi-sid: efuse write is disabled in this build (hardening)\n");
+		err = -EPERM;
+		goto _out;
 		addr = (uintptr_t)nfcr->key_store.key_data;
 		if (copy_from_user(nfcr->temp_data,
 				   (void *)addr,
